          lemma: "ἐν παραβολαῖς … ὁ σπείρων",
          title: "Why parables — and a sower who wastes seed",
          body: "A **παραβολή** is literally a *throwing-alongside*: one thing laid next to another so that meaning arcs across the gap. It is not a sermon illustration (decoration for a point already stated) but the point itself, delivered in a form that cannot be received passively — a parable does nothing until the hearer works on it, which means it does nothing *to* the hearer who won't. That selectivity is its genius and its offence, and the chapter will address it head-on (vv. 11-15). The first parable is about parables. Look at the sower's technique: he throws seed on the path, on rock, into thorns — a first-century farmer sowed *before* ploughing, so this broadcast onto unpromising ground is real practice, but the parable makes it a portrait of scandalous indiscriminateness. The sower is not running a soil survey first. The same handful goes to every square metre, including the square metres any consultant would have written off — which is, of course, the ministry Matthew has been describing for twelve chapters: word and healing scattered on Pharisees, tax collectors, fishermen, crowds who will shout for crucifixion. The parable quietly moves the question from the sower's efficiency to the hearer's ground: the seed is uniform; the yields are not; and the one variable left in the story is you.",
